00:09:35,160 --> 00:09:39,140
And at the end of high school, he ended up entering a competition

247
00:09:39,140 --> 00:09:41,580
that was held by Microsoft and ,

248
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:45,060
which is pretty much through Microsoft Excel.

249
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:47,640
And he went all the way to the world competition and competed

250
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,520
in a world championship in Microsoft Excel.

251
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:51,000
And out of 2 million people,

252
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,920
he became the number one Microsoft Excel user

253
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:53,600
in the world.

254
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:54,720
It's crazy.

255
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:55,680
It's crazy.

256
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:57,560
Listen, listen, you got to look it up.

257
00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:58,300
I'm telling you.

258
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:00,440
Jack Dumoulin, Microsoft Excel, this is the number one user

259
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:01,840
of Microsoft Excel in the world.

260
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:03,640
I'm serious.

261
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:04,940
It's a real thing.

262
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:07,360
But through the, I'm serious.

263
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:09,920
It's a real thing.

264
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:12,420
It's one of the craziest stories ever.

265
00:10:12,420 --> 00:10:15,460
Like, smartest kid I know, I promise you.

266
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:18,620
And, but through that, it opened up so many opportunities

267
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:19,640
and doors for him.

268
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:22,000
He decided to ultimately, like I said, we're from DC.

269
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,720
We moved out to, or he decided to move out to Austin

270
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:26,680
and go to UT.

271
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:29,880
But at the same time, he opened up his own, yeah,

272
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:31,680
welcome, yeah, he opened up his own,

273
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:33,520
kind of like tech consulting company

274
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:35,120
where he was building out, you know,

275
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:37,840
softwares and integrating that with Excel for companies

276
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:39,040
in different states.

277
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:40,900
And he also led to him having the opportunity.

278
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:42,000
He was pretty much mentored.

279
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,680
Everybody knows Bucky's, right?

280
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,480
So Bucky, one of the CEOs, one of the owners of Bucky's,

281
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:48,360
mentored him for about two years.

282
00:10:48,360 --> 00:10:53,360
And so for myself personally, you know, I was behind them.

283
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:54,800
He was already out in Austin.

284
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:56,560
And it was around when the pandemic hit

285
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:58,220
and we really saw kind of the emergence

286
00:10:58,220 --> 00:10:59,880
of various social media platforms,

287
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:01,320
and in particular TikTok.

288
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:03,720
And I grew really fascinated about how

289
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:05,640
there's all these people that are, you know,

290
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:08,240
finding their passion and they're able to monetize it

291
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:09,940
through just posting content around that.

292
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:12,080
I mean, I was seeing how the creator economy

293
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:14,180
was growing valuations in the next five years,

294
00:11:14,180 --> 00:11:15,760
it's going to be over a hundred billion dollars.

295
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:17,920
And I was like, hey, this is something I could do.

296
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,660
I studied the algorithms and I was able to grow

297
00:11:19,660 --> 00:11:21,540
my personal TikTok following to right around

298
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:24,280
a million followers in less than a year.

299
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:26,680
And, you know, through that, you know,

300
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:29,680
as I mentioned before, we all grew up in the DC area.

301
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:31,360
The thing about DC is it's a very strict

302
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:32,300
and structured city.

303
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:34,040
It's very centered around government jobs,

304
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:35,400
contracting, defense.

305
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:37,320
And I tell people, you want to work for Congress,

306
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,940
you want to go into politics, DC is the place to be.

307
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:42,040
But if you're entrepreneurial and have the desire

308
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:43,940
to start your own business, you got to get out of DC.

309
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:46,080
You got to get into a collaborative environment

310
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:47,900
like Austin, where everyone's looking to provide value

311
00:11:47,900 --> 00:11:48,740
for one another.

312
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:51,860
So that kind of us having a similar mindset in that sense

313
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:54,720
is kind of what led to a further spark in our relationship.

314
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:59,100
So how it all kind of came together is late 2020,

315
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:01,360
we met at a mutual friend's house and really

316
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:03,120
reconnected over a couple of years.

317
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:04,920
It's been years since we've seen each other.

318
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:09,020
And we realized we had a lot of similar interests and goals,

319
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:10,420
and we knew we wanted to work on something,

320
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:12,560
but we weren't sure what it was going to be.

321
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:15,780
So the only problem with that is that Jack and James

322
00:12:15,780 --> 00:12:19,180
were about to move to Texas permanently a week later.

323
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:22,220
So what we had to do is I was thinking of ideas

324
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:24,980
of what we could do in a week or shortly after they moved

325
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:27,680
to Texas, I called them and I pitched them on starting

326
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:31,000
an e-sports and gaming channel and they agreed.

327
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:34,160
And we started that and we grew it to about 10,000 followers

328
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:35,520
after a couple of months.

329
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:38,000
And even though the channel was going well,

330
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,780
it wasn't something we were all passionate about.

331
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:41,140
So we decided to pivot.

332
00:12:41,140 --> 00:12:43,600
And so what we decided to do is we all have a passion

333
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:44,980
and interest in business.

334
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:47,260
And we wanted to share some of the lessons that we've learned

335
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:50,380
along the way from our mentors and just share ideas

336
00:12:50,380 --> 00:12:51,800
with other people.

337
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:54,840
But we soon realized that people don't want to learn

338
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:56,960
about business from some young 20 year old guys,

339
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:58,520
no one knows who they are.

340
00:12:58,520 --> 00:13:01,120
So we had to make another change.

341
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:03,360
And that's when we started to do street interviews.

342
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:06,780
And we noticed after the first interview that we posted,

343
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:09,760
our channel really started to get some really good traction.

344
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:12,780
And so then we saw the greater vision for the company

345
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:14,640
and we wanted to seek out the best of the best.

346
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:16,140
And that's how we've been able to get interviews

347
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:18,760
like Mark Cuban, CEO of HopDotty and others.

348
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:23,760
And that's where we were really just able to grow the channel

349
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:25,740
and it really took off after that point

350
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:28,060
once we started doing the interviews.

351
00:13:28,060 --> 00:13:28,900
100%.

352
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:31,320
And so looking back at our journey of how that kind of

353
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:33,420
brought us together to start the School of Hard Knocks,

354
00:13:33,420 --> 00:13:36,020
which is the name of our channel,

355
00:13:36,020 --> 00:13:37,600
we were able to comprise some of the lessons

356
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:39,480
that we actually learned from content creation.

357
00:13:39,480 --> 00:13:41,320
And we want to bring that to you today.

358
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:43,540
And so we have three major things.

359
00:13:43,540 --> 00:13:46,400
The first being is that when you want to create content

360
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:49,840
and grow your brand, you need to find your why.

361
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:51,920
And although find your why might sound kind of cliche,

362
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:54,180
like, oh, find your why, what even is that?

363
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:56,180
The reason why I say it's important is because

364
00:13:56,180 --> 00:13:57,980
if you don't have a why, if you don't have a mission

365
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:00,620
for why you want to create content or grow your brand,

366
00:14:00,620 --> 00:14:01,360
there's going to, you're going to run into a lot of people

367
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:04,120
who are going to run into two things as a content creator

368
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:05,820
that stumbles every kind of creator early on

369
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:06,860
in their journey.

370
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:08,500
The first of that being is getting discouraged

371
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:10,840
with views, likes, followers, the numbers.

372
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:13,260
You get discouraged if you post a video

373
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:14,360
and it just doesn't do well.

374
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:15,720
James actually gave me great advice on this

375
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,120
while he was growing his brand.

376
00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:20,120
Yeah, and this is something that I really tried to instill

377
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:21,740
kind of in our mindset when we were going forward

378
00:14:21,740 --> 00:14:23,820
with Hard Knocks is that when you're starting out,

379
00:14:23,820 --> 00:14:25,120
when you're getting out of your comfort zone,

380
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:27,860
you may not be used to putting yourself behind a camera,

381
00:14:27,860 --> 00:14:29,880
putting that content out to where potentially

382
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:31,820
hundreds and thousands of people can see it.

383
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:33,760
And, you know, therefore, you really just have to,

384
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:35,200
like he said, you can't get discouraged.

385
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:36,860
You can't care about what other people think.

386
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:37,840
You just have to go for it.

387
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:38,740
You know, if you have the vision,

388
00:14:38,740 --> 00:14:41,100
if you see the potential in it, just send it, go for it.

389
00:14:41,100 --> 00:14:42,980
And I'm telling you, as long as you remember

390
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,380
why you're doing it and the greater purpose,

391
00:14:44,380 --> 00:14:46,180
it's going to pay off.

392
00:14:46,180 --> 00:14:47,020
Exactly.

393
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:49,340
So the first thing being you have to overcome

394
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:50,560
being discouraged about views.

395
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:52,440
You could put one piece of content,

396
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:54,320
three pieces of content, five pieces of content.

397
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:55,460
It doesn't do well.

398
00:14:55,460 --> 00:14:57,740
If you ultimately decide to give up and quit

399
00:14:57,740 --> 00:14:59,860
just because not enough people saw your content at first,

400
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:01,820
then you're never going to be able to start a channel.

401
00:15:01,820 --> 00:15:04,560
For us in particular, for the School of Hard Knocks,

402
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:06,180
when we started doing business content,

403
00:15:06,180 --> 00:15:09,380
we had put out 200 pieces of content on our Instagram

404
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:12,120
for over a month and only had about 35,

405
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:13,320
40 followers to begin with.

406
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:15,880
That's not a lot of support for putting out 200 pieces

407
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:18,120
of good quality content.

408
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:21,280
And so if you don't have your why, you're just going to quit.

409
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:23,680
The second being is you can't be afraid of failure.

410
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:26,640
You can't be afraid of people judging you

411
00:15:26,640 --> 00:15:27,520
when you get on camera.

412
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:29,560
You can't be afraid of what they think of you

413
00:15:29,560 --> 00:15:32,380
because if you ultimately have your mission, your why,

414
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:34,580
and why you're making this great content for people,

415
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:35,860
then that stuff's not going to matter to you.

416
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:37,620
For us in particular, when we went up and interviewed

417
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:40,120
Mark Cuban, we actually saw him at South by Southwest

418
00:15:40,120 --> 00:15:42,000
just after he gave a talk on the street.

419
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,160
If we didn't have the courage to go up and ask him and say,

420
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:45,880
hey, we run this channel.

421
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:47,760
We want to give back to the younger generation.

422
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:48,600
Could we ask you a question?

423
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,060
What advice would you tell your younger self?

424
00:15:51,060 --> 00:15:53,520
If we were so afraid of getting rejected by him,

425
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:55,160
then that video never would have gotten posted,

426
00:15:55,160 --> 00:15:57,080
never would have gotten the millions of views that it did

427
00:15:57,080 --> 00:15:58,580
and the people that it impacted.

428
00:15:58,580 --> 00:15:59,560
When we saw the impact

429
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:01,420
of videos like our interview with Mark Cuban,

430
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:03,800
we quickly realized that our impact spread

431
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:05,020
beyond the United States.

432
00:16:05,020 --> 00:16:09,580
Over 50% of our 1.1 million following is outside of the US.

433
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:11,800
And we even had people reaching out to us and saying,

434
00:16:11,800 --> 00:16:14,960
hey, I just started my dream business on the side of my job

435
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:17,940
because of a video that we saw.

436
00:16:18,780 --> 00:16:22,060
We even saw that people were starting to save money.

437
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:24,260
And so that ultimately is why you need to find your why.

438
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:27,080
For us, we wanted to change the way that financial literacy

439
00:16:27,080 --> 00:16:29,080
and career mentorship is taught in schools.

440
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:31,940
We also had great mentors around us.

441
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:33,440
And we noticed that a lot of our peers didn't.

442
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:35,120
And we wanted to bring that through our content.

443
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:36,520
But ultimately, if things aren't going well,

444
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:38,300
you can always change it up.

445
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:41,700
Just like in business, in content is exactly the same.

446
00:16:41,700 --> 00:16:44,540
Is if things aren't working out, you need to pivot.

447
00:16:44,540 --> 00:16:47,840
And so in our business, we had to pivot twice.

448
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:50,080
Like I said, we started as an e-sports company,

449
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:51,420
switched to business.

450
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:53,520
People don't want to learn business from young guys,

451
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:55,260
switched it again to interviews.

452
00:16:55,260 --> 00:16:58,180
And this is where things finally started to click.

453
00:16:58,180 --> 00:16:59,400
And if things aren't working out for you,

454
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:01,840
you're posting consistently and it's not working,

455
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:03,200
here are some things that I would recommend

456
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:05,000
that you can consider doing.

457
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,800
The first thing would be change up the delivery

458
00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:08,820
of the actual content.

459
00:17:08,820 --> 00:17:10,840
Change up your storytelling and how you're approaching

460
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:12,040
the content and the viewer.

461
00:17:12,040 --> 00:17:12,980
And that's the number one thing,

462
00:17:12,980 --> 00:17:15,260
is you actually have to focus on the viewer.

463
00:17:15,260 --> 00:17:18,380
And the second thing, also to add onto that, is the editing.

464
00:17:18,380 --> 00:17:19,980
The second thing you should consider doing

465
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:22,880
is changing your niche or topic that you're talking about.

466
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:24,780
Let's say you're talking about business overall.

467
00:17:24,780 --> 00:17:26,780
Maybe you need to go a little bit deeper

468
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:28,660
and go into a sector like marketing.

469
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:31,160
And then you can go a little bit deeper

470
00:17:31,160 --> 00:17:32,460
and talk about that.

471
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:34,060
The third thing I would recommend doing

472
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:35,600
is if things still aren't working out,

473
00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:37,720
is model someone that is successful

474
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:40,600
within what you want to do and model after them.

475
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:42,680
But ultimately, the most important thing you can do

476
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:44,160
is be consistent.

477
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:47,080
Hands down, for us in particular,

478
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:49,600
the absolute most valuable trait and thing

479
00:17:49,600 --> 00:17:52,360
that we brought into our company and through our content

480
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:54,400
is just being consistent.

481
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:55,980
Early on, you don't have a lot of followers,

482
00:17:55,980 --> 00:17:57,080
you don't have a huge base.

483
00:17:57,080 --> 00:17:58,640
It's all about volume early on.

484
00:17:58,640 --> 00:18:01,360
You've got to be putting out numerous pieces of content.

485
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:03,840
I love to give this analogy whenever I'm talking to people.

486
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:06,000
You have person A and you have person B.

487
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,900
Person A is getting into content,

488
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:09,060
he's going through the motions, he's like,

489
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:11,200
hey, I'm putting out a couple posts a week.

490
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:12,360
And then you have person B.

491
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:14,960
Person B, he's like, hey, this is my structured

492
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:16,400
posting schedule, I'm gonna be putting up

493
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:18,400
three to four pieces of content a day.

494
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:19,580
When you look at the end of a week,

495
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:22,280
that person's putting up 20 to 30 pieces of content

496
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:23,300
as opposed to the other person

497
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:24,720
just going through the motions.

498
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:26,560
That person who's putting up all that content

499
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:28,400
is gonna beat that person 10 times out of 10 times.

500
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:31,680
Yeah, I even saw this with James' personal journey

501
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:33,000
when he started posting content.

502
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,480
Every single day, he was posting three, four times a day

503
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:37,540
for 60 days straight.

